; model_copy_price_refused: one model copy has one price, and this is the
; budget one byte below it.
;
; spec/xqvm/SPEC.md's Allocation budget section states the rule: copying a
; model costs its declared size at the variable rate plus one linear entry
; per live linear coefficient and one quadratic entry per live quadratic
; one -- exactly what the allocator and the coefficient writes charged to
; build it. The same number is the charge wherever the copy happens, so a
; program cannot pick a cheap route to the same duplication.
;
; Sizing. BQMX of size 4 charges 4 * 8 = 32. One SETLINE on a model
; register charges one linear entry, 32. OUTPUT then copies the whole
; model across the host boundary and is charged model_bytes = 4 * 8 + 32 =
; 64, taking the run to 128. A budget of 127 admits everything up to the
; copy and refuses the copy itself, so the fault lands on OUTPUT and pins
; the copy price from below.
;
; This vector exists because the model copy rate had no cross-
; implementation pin at all (QUI-1163). It was asserted by two crate-local
; Rust tests and by nothing on the Python side, against a schedule whose
; whole point is that both implementations charge the same. Its sibling
; model_copy_price_admitted pins the same number from above; neither alone
; pins a number, since `expected.json` asserts faults and outputs rather
; than bytes.
;
; The copy is priced over the live coefficient map rather than the
; declared size alone, which is why the SETLINE is here: without it the
; copy would cost 32 and the vector would not distinguish the model rate
; from the bare variable rate.

PUSH 4
BQMX r0          ; charges 4 * VARIABLE_BYTES = 32

PUSH 0
PUSH 1
SETLINE r0       ; charges LINEAR_ENTRY_BYTES = 32, total 64

PUSH 0
OUTPUT r0        ; wants model_bytes = 64, total 128, refused at 127
HALT
